attempt1

	This is a proof why "reduce left until some character is missing,
	reduce right to restore the balance" does not work.

	aabaaaacaab[c]lr	a=8	b=2	c=2	k=2
	aabaaaacaa[b]l[c]r	a=8	b=2	c=1	k=2	--c	--left
	aabaaaacaa[b]lrc	a=8	b=2	c=2	k=2	++c	--right
	aabaaaaca[a]l[b]rc	a=8	b=1	c=2	k=2	--b	--left
	aabaaaaca[a]lrbrc	a=8	b=0	c=2	k=2	--b	--right

	Track it. See v1.

attempt2

	[aab]aaaa[caabc]

	Find a substring with a=4 b=0 c=0.

	Overlooked is a=4. For totala-k = 6.

	Track this version. See leetcode.

	printf("totala-k=%d totalb-k=%d totalc-k=%d\n", totala-k, totalb-k, totalc-k);
	printf("xminutes=%d left=%ld right=%ld\n", minutes, left-s, right-s);

attempt3

	attempt1 is valid. left=right breaks everything.

	[]aa[baaaacaabc]
	[a]a[baaaacaabc]
	[aa][baaaacaabc]
	[aab]aaaa[caabc]
	[aaba]aaa[caabc]
	[aabaa]aa[caabc]
	[aabaaa]a[caabc]
	[aabaaaa][caabc]
	[aabaaaac]aa[bc]

	Insight: right always goes to the right.
